{
    "background_story": "Kolson was a free-spirited musician who called Glasgow home. Growing up in the city's vibrant West End, he was surrounded by the sounds of traditional Scottish folk music and the hum of the city's energetic streets. As a teenager, Kolson began busking on Buchanan Street, his soulful voice and skilled guitar playing quickly drawing crowds. He became a fixture on the Glasgow music scene, playing sold-out shows at King Tut's Wah Wah Hut and the Barrowland Ballroom. Kolson's music was infused with the spirit of Glasgow, reflecting the city's rich history, cultural diversity, and resilience. His song 'River Clyde Lullaby' became an anthem for the city, capturing the hearts of Glaswegians and visitors alike. Kolson's love for Glasgow inspired a new generation of musicians, cementing his legacy as a champion of the city's thriving music scene.",
